### Modern switch Expressions

Explore Java 14+ switch expressions: arrow (->) syntax, multiple comma-separated labels, and yielding values directly.

Classic switch statements in Java were inherited from C in the 1990s and suffered from two major flaws: accidental fallthrough bugs caused by missing `break`, and verbosity because switch was a statement (it did work) rather than an expression (it could not return a value). Starting with Java 14, Java introduced **switch expressions** with arrow syntax (`->`), revolutionizing modern Java control flow.

---

### 1. Arrow Syntax (case ->): Safe & Concise

In modern switch, you replace the colon `:` with an arrow `->`. When a case matches, ONLY the expression or block to the right of the arrow executes. **There is no fallthrough**, and no `break` statement is needed:

```java
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("Tuesday");
    case 3 -> System.out.println("Wednesday");
    default -> System.out.println("Other Day");
}
```

---

### 2. switch as an Expression: Returning Values Directly

A switch can now return a value directly into a variable assignment:

```java
String dayName = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    case 3 -> "Wednesday";
    default -> "Unknown";
};
```

Notice the closing semicolon `;` after the curly brace. Because this is a variable assignment statement (`String dayName = switch (...) ;`), the semicolon is mandatory.

---

### 3. Comma-Separated Multiple Case Labels

Instead of stacking vertical case statements to share logic, modern switch allows clean comma-separated values:

```java
String typeOfDay = switch (day) {
    case 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 -> "Weekday";
    case 6, 7 -> "Weekend";
    default -> "Invalid Day";
};
```

---

### 4. Multi-Line Blocks & the `yield` Keyword

If a branch requires multiple lines of logic (such as logging or calculations) before returning a value, wrap the branch in curly braces `{}` and return the value using the `yield` keyword:

```java
int discountPercent = switch (customerTier) {
    case "REGULAR" -> 5;
    case "PREMIUM" -> 15;
    case "VIP" -> {
        System.out.println("Applying VIP Gold perk!");
        int base = 20;
        yield base + 5; // Returns 25
    }
    default -> 0;
};
```

`yield` is a contextual keyword specifically designed for switch expressions to distinguish returning from a switch vs returning from an entire method with `return`.

---

### 5. Compiler-Enforced Exhaustiveness

When used as an expression (assigning to a variable), the switch **must be exhaustive** — meaning every possible value of the evaluated type must be covered. For primitive integers and Strings, a `default` branch is strictly mandatory, otherwise the code will fail to compile.

---

> [!NOTE]
> **Key Takeaway**:
> Modern Java 14+ switch expressions replace clumsy break statements with clean arrow `->` syntax, allow comma-separated cases, enforce exhaustiveness, and yield values directly into variables.

activities.codeSnippet:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String plan = "PRO";

        // Modern switch expression returning monthly cost
        int monthlyCost = switch (plan) {
            case "FREE" -> 0;
            case "BASIC" -> 9;
            case "PRO" -> {
                int baseRate = 29;
                int storageAddon = 10;
                yield baseRate + storageAddon;
            }
            case "ENTERPRISE" -> 99;
            default -> -1;
        };

        System.out.println("Plan: " + plan + " | Monthly Cost: $" + monthlyCost);
    }
}

